digraph ReasoningTree {
  rankdir="TB";
  "I3" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction: Given texturing achieves both properties through microstructure optimization (phenomenon) AND microstructure affects poling/switching (knowledge), therefore microstructure optimization is key."];
  "I8" [label="(0,0,0) Induction: Given single techniques have limited effects (case) AND combining techniques addresses multiple defects (common pattern), therefore synergistic combination should be more effective."];
  "N11" [label="(12,0,0) High sintering temperature (~1200°C) and Pb volatilization generate pores and oxygen vacancies in random PZT ceramics."];
  "N12" [label="(13,0,0) Pores reduce density and breakdown strength; oxygen vacancies create dipole defects; both hinder saturated poling and domain switching."];
  "N13" [label="(14,0,0) Dipole defects pin domain switching at room temperature and de-pin at high temperature, worsening both piezoelectricity and temperature stability."];
  "N14" [label="(15,0,0) Eliminating pores and reducing oxygen vacancies should improve both piezoelectricity and temperature stability."];
  "N16" [label="(16,0,0) The new strategy should be more economical and practical than texturing."];
  "N2" [label="(2,0,0) Technology demands piezoceramics with better overall performance for harsh conditions."];
  "N20" [label="(31,0,0) Most literature used single process techniques with limited enhancement."];
  "N21" [label="(32,0,0) Hot-press sintering moderately improves density and properties."];
  "N22" [label="(33,0,0) Tape casting improves microstructure and is used for various ceramic fabrication."];
  "N23" [label="(34,0,0) Tape casting alone hardly improves both properties simultaneously without texturing templates."];
  "N24" [label="(0,0,0) Implicit: If combining multiple process techniques addresses multiple defects, then synergistic effects are possible."];
  "N3" [label="(3,0,0) Piezoelectric devices require excellent piezoelectric properties AND good temperature stability."];
  "N5" [label="(7,0,0) Rule: High piezoelectricity requires structural instability; high temperature stability requires stable structure."];
  "N6" [label="(8,0,0) Strategies introducing structural instability inevitably decrease Curie temperature (Tc), limiting workable temperature range."];
  "N8" [label="(9,0,0) Texturing method enhanced both piezoelectricity and temperature stability without increasing Tc, despite high cost and complexity."];
  "N9" [label="(10,0,0) High texturing degree and large grain size enable sufficient domain switching and saturated poling, resulting in stable phase structure."];
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) This work aims to investigate Pb0.92Ba0.08[Zr0.50+xTi0.48−x(Nb0.5Sb0.5)0.02]O3 ceramics by adjusting the Zr/Ti ratio for morphotropic phase-boundary/phase-boundary engineering and combining tape-casting, lamination, and hot-pressing process engineering to reduce pores and oxygen vacancies, with the goal of simultaneously enhancing piezoelectric performance and temperature stability.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) When piezoceramic applications require both strong electromechanical response and thermal reliability, and separate strategies address only parts of the trade-off, an integrated design that coordinates phase structure and processing defects is scientifically motivated."];
  "R3" [label="(0,0,0) Technology and device requirements motivate developing PZT piezoceramics that combine strong piezoelectric properties with good temperature stability."];
  "R4" [label="(0,0,0) Because high piezoelectricity and high temperature stability favor conflicting structural conditions, strategies that add structural instability but lower Curie temperature expose a trade-off challenge."];
  "R5" [label="(0,0,0) Pores and oxygen vacancies generated in random PZT ceramics can hinder saturated poling and domain switching, thereby degrading ceramic piezoelectric behavior."];
  "R7" [label="(0,0,0) Since dipole defects worsen domain switching and temperature-dependent stability, eliminating pores and reducing oxygen vacancies is a plausible route to improve both piezoelectricity and temperature stability."];
  "R8" [label="(0,0,0) The high cost and complexity of effective texturing motivate seeking a more economical and practical microstructure-optimization strategy."];
